## Why This Is Asked

This problem tests interval DP and both memoization and tabulation approaches. It asks for precise state definitions, correct recurrence when ends match, and careful base cases. It also probes understanding of time/space trade-offs for cubic-time DP.

## Key Concepts

- DP on substrings
- Memoization vs tabulation
- Interval DP with matching ends

## Code Example

```javascript
function minPalRemoval(s){
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n}, ()=> Array(n).fill(0));
  for(let i=n-1;i>=0;i--){
    dp[i][i] = 1;
    for(let j=i+1;j<n;j++){
      dp[i][j] = 1 + dp[i+1][j];
      for(let k=i+1;k<=j;k++){
        if(s[i] === s[k]){
          const left = (k-1 >= i+1) ? dp[i+1][k-1] : 0;
          const right = (k+1 <= j) ? dp[k+1][j] : 0;
          dp[i][j] = Math.min(dp[i][j], left + right);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return n ? dp[0][n-1] : 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you count distinct removal sequences?
- Can you optimize memory to O(n) per row with rolling arrays?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP state design, memoization vs tabulation, and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions; state i is start index; transitions extend end while sum <= limit.
- Prefix sums for O(1) range sums; reconstruction via choice pointers.
- Edge cases: impossible when an element exceeds limit; handling ties.

## Code Example
```javascript
function minPartitionCost(nums, limit){
  const n = nums.length, pref=[0];
  for(const x of nums) pref.push(pref[pref.length-1]+x);
  const memo = new Array(n+1).fill(null);
  function dfs(i){
    if(i===n) return [0,[]];
    if(memo[i]) return memo[i];
    let best=[Infinity, null];
    for(let j=i;j<n;j++){ const sum=pref[j+1]-pref[i]; if(sum>limit) break; const sub=dfs(j+1); const cost=(j-i+1)**2+sub[0]; if(cost<best[0]) best=[cost, [i,j].concat(sub[1]||[])]; }
    memo[i]=best; return best;
  }
  return dfs(0);
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you modify to also return the actual blocks boundaries when multiple optimal partitions exist?
- How does the solution change if limit is dynamic per block?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical sorting algorithm design that balances cache efficiency with worst-case performance guarantees. Hybrid approaches like this are commonly used in real-world systems where both average-case speed and theoretical bounds matter.

## Key Concepts
- **Hybrid sorting**: Combines partitioning (QuickSort) with stable merging (MergeSort)
- **Introsort-like depth limiting**: Prevents pathological cases
- **In-place stable merging**: Achieved through array rotations
- **Cache-friendly recursive structure**: Larger partitions processed first
- **Complexity**: Average/worst case O(n log n), additional space O(log n) for recursion stack

## Code Implementation
```javascript
function hybridSort(arr, left = 0, right = arr.length - 1, depth = 0) {
  const n = right - left + 1;
  const threshold = 32;
  const maxDepth = 2 * Math.floor(Math.log2(n));

  if (n <= 1) return;

  if (n <= threshold || depth >= maxDepth) {
    // Bottom-up in-place stable merge sort
    mergeSortInPlace(arr, left, right);
    return;
  }

  // QuickSort with median-of-three pivot
  const pivotIndex = medianOfThree(arr, left, right);
  const partitionIndex = partition(arr, left, right, pivotIndex);

  // Recurse on larger partition first to bound stack depth
  const leftSize = partitionIndex - left;
  const rightSize = right - partitionIndex;

  if (leftSize > rightSize) {
    hybridSort(arr, left, partitionIndex - 1, depth + 1);
    hybridSort(arr, partitionIndex + 1, right, depth + 1);
  } else {
    hybridSort(arr, partitionIndex + 1, right, depth + 1);
    hybridSort(arr, left, partitionIndex - 1, depth + 1);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you empirically determine the optimal threshold size?
- What techniques would you use to verify the algorithm's stability?
- How does this hybrid approach perform on partially sorted data?
- What modifications would be needed for parallel execution?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to combine hash tables with heaps for real-time top-K queries under sliding-window expirations. It evaluates understanding of lazy cleanup techniques, time-based eviction strategies, and maintaining data consistency under concurrent updates.

## Key Concepts
- Hash map for O(1) task lookups and metadata storage
- Max-heap for efficient top-K ordering
- Lazy deletion to avoid expensive per-update heap removals
- Time-based expiration using timestamp validation

## Code Example
```javascript
class Leaderboard {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.map = new Map(); // task_id -> {score, timestamp}
    this.heap = new MaxHeap(); // (score, task_id)
  }

  update(task_id, delta, ts) {
    const current = this.map.get(task_id) || { score: 0, timestamp: 0 };
    const updated = { 
      score: current.score + delta, 
      timestamp: ts 
    };
    this.map.set(task_id, updated);
    this.heap.push([updated.score, task_id]);
  }

  getTopK(k, current_ts) {
    const result = [];
    const temp = [];

    while (result.length < k && !this.heap.isEmpty()) {
      const [score, task_id] = this.heap.pop();
      const task = this.map.get(task_id);

      if (task && task.score === score && current_ts - task.timestamp <= this.W) {
        result.push({ task_id, score });
      } else {
        temp.push([score, task_id]);
      }
    }

    // Restore invalid entries
    temp.forEach(entry => this.heap.push(entry));

    return result;
  }
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- **Update**: O(1) hash map + O(log n) heap push = O(log n)
- **Top-K query**: O(k log n) worst case due to lazy deletion
- **Space**: O(n) for hash map + heap (potentially O(n + m) where m are stale entries)
- **Expiration**: Handled during queries via timestamp validation

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses dynamic programming fundamentals through practical string segmentation, testing understanding of both top-down memoization and bottom-up tabulation approaches.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic Programming: memoization vs. tabulation
- String matching optimization (startsWith, trie)
- Time and space trade-offs between DP approaches
- State definition and transition logic

## Code Example
```javascript
// Top-down memoized solution
function minWordSegmentsTopDown(s, dict) {
  const n = s.length;
  const memo = Array(n + 1).fill(-1);
  memo[n] = 0;

  function dfs(i) {
    if (memo[i] !== -1) return memo[i];
    let minSegs = Infinity;
    for (const word of dict) {
      if (s.startsWith(word, i)) {
        const result = dfs(i + word.length);
        if (result !== -1) {
          minSegs = Math.min(minSegs, 1 + result);
        }
      }
    }
    memo[i] = minSegs === Infinity ? -1 : minSegs;
    return memo[i];
  }
  return dfs(0);
}

// Bottom-up tabulated solution
function minWordSegmentsBottomUp(s, dict) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array(n + 1).fill(Infinity);
  dp[n] = 0;

  for (let i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    for (const word of dict) {
      if (s.startsWith(word, i)) {
        if (dp[i + word.length] !== Infinity) {
          dp[i] = Math.min(dp[i], 1 + dp[i + word.length]);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[0] === Infinity ? -1 : dp[0];
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- **Time:** O(n × m × L) where n = |s|, m = dict size, L = avg word length. Using a Trie reduces this to O(n × L).
- **Space:** O(n) for the memo/DP array.
